            <ArticleTitle>Integrating complementary and conventional symptom management in a cancer center.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>25-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>One goal of oncology nursing is to help patients achieve the best possible quality of life. In the conventional care perspective, quality of life has four aspects: physical, emotional, social, and spiritual. From the complementary care perspective, it is defined as harmony of body, mind, and spirit. Integrating these perspectives of quality of life allows us to have a fuller and richer view of the patient, accentuating the core of his or her being: values, beliefs, and goals. The Pain and Symptom Management Service has embraced this integrated view and, by doing so, has improved symptom management outcomes for patients and their families.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
